    "body": "Third of the stack, on top of #2494. A signature may declare `type T` with no definition; a module sealed by it must define `T`, but outside the module `T` is known only as the path `M.T`.\n\n- **Syntax.** `type T` is a signature item (`SigTypeAbstract`) in the grammar, the tile forms (listed before the manifest form so that `type` typed in a signature expands to it), `MakeTerm`, the Menhir `sigItem` rule, the printers, equality, abbreviation and ExplainThis. In the editor, typing `=` after a bare `type T` upgrades the tile to `type T = \u2026` (`Insert.upgrade_bare_sig_type`); there is no downgrade.\n- **Sealing.** A module analyzed against `{ type T; let x : T }` must define `T` (a missing definition is a `ModuleMissingMembers`; a value named `T` does not count), and its members are checked against its own definition. `Typ.sig_sub` realizes an abstract member of the required signature by whatever the module provides; a manifest member is not satisfied by an abstract one. `Typ.meet` matches abstract members only with abstract members.\n- **Path types.** An abstract member projected from a module path is the stuck normal form `M.T`, consistent with itself and `?` only. `Typ.path_sig` now returns the path along with the items; `weak_head_normalize`, `normalize` and `meet` stop at a stuck path. A module variable's type is strengthened (`Typ.strengthen`, at `Var`, the capitalized-constructor fallback and `Dot`), so `M.x : M.T` and `module N = M` shares `M.T`, while two modules sealed by the same signature have distinct types. Only a path can name an abstract type: projecting from any other expression, or through a signature alias, gives `?`. The cursor inspector reports `M.T` as an abstract type (`Message.PathAbstract`).\n- **Dynamics.** Abstract members have no runtime content. Runtime sealing now ascribes each kept member to its closed type, so an abstract member casts to `?` (previously a bare `T` left stuck casts such as `0:T`).\n\nTests: eighteen `Statics.Modules` cases (sealing, path types, aliasing, distinct sealings, transparency, missing and kind mismatches, forward references), `Typ.SigPaths`, `Evaluator.Modules`, `Editing.SigAbstract` (bare form, `=` upgrade, Cmd+D), plus MakeTerm, Menhir, round-trip, TyDi and Equality cases. Slide section F (a `Counter` module) and `docs/modules.md` describe the design.\n\nKnown, planned for the next PR: `fun (m : { type T; let x : T }) -> m.x` currently types as `S -> m.T` with `m` escaping its scope; the generative `Typ.avoid` handles that.\n\n## Known gaps\n\n- **No sharing constraint.** There is no `with type` spelling, so a signature\n  cannot be refined by fixing one member, and nothing says the equivalent of\n  `SHOW with T = Int`. The workaround is a hand-written signature literal\n  carrying a manifest member, which is verbose and easy to get wrong; the\n  implicits part (#2508) documents it because resolution needs it often.\n- **No opaque-versus-transparent ascription.** One colon seals, and a sealed\n  module's type members can never be selected by an argument type, so a\n  sealed implicit instance is unusable. That is documentation today rather\n  than a language distinction; a second spelling that checks against a\n  signature while keeping manifest types would remove the footgun.\n- **Type members cannot take parameters.** The type-pattern grammar admits a\n  bare name, so nothing abstracting over a type constructor is expressible:\n  no collection, monad or functor signature. Value members may be\n  polymorphic, so signatures in the style of show, equality and monoid are\n  unaffected.\n\n## Verification\n\n`make dev` clean \u00b7 `make test-quick` green (3600 tests).\n\n## Follow-up commits\n\n- **Report a missing member once, and let signatures name sibling members.** The synthetic `x : T` expectation checked inside a module body no longer shares ids with the signature's type nodes, and is skipped when it mentions a name the module does not define, so a missing `type T` is reported only on the module. Signature items can now see earlier value and module members, so `{ module Inner : { type T }; let y : Inner.T }` is well-formed; in subtyping a sibling is bound to the type the module provides.\n- **The Modules slide is split here** rather than in part 4: sections E and F move to `module-signatures.hz`, because statics recursion depth grows with the `let` chain and the combined slide was at the JavaScript stack limit (jsoo overflows node's default stack at roughly 100 nested lets).\n- **An abstract member cannot be named through a signature alias.** `type S = { type T; \u2026 } in let y : S.T = \u2026` used to type `S.T` as `?`, so any value passed and the inspector called `?` an abstract type. The alias names no module, so there is no particular `T` to name: it is now an error on the label (`TypAbstractMemberOfSignature`), with the dot explaining that a module `M` of the signature names it as `M.T`; TyDi no longer suggests such members. Manifest members through an alias, and abstract members through a module path, are unchanged. A type path rooted at a base type name (`Nat.T`) now says the name is a base type, not a module. Slide item 36a.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\n\n\n",
